.\" keyfilterctl(1) -- manual page
.TH KEYFILTERCTL 1 "November 2026" "KeyFilter 0.01" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
keyfilterctl \- control the running instances of keyfilter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B keyfilterctl
.RB [ \-\-pid
.IR PID ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B keyfilterctl
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B keyfilterctl
talks to the instances of
.BR keyfilter (1)
that run for the current user, through their sockets in
.IR ~/.config/keyfilter/sockets .
.PP
It is the same file as
.BR keyfilter ,
started under the name
.BR keyfilterctl .
A symbolic link is enough:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ln \-s keyfilter keyfilterctl
.fi
.RE
.PP
The commands that talk to one instance use the only one that runs, or the one chosen with
.BR \-\-pid .
An instance is a socket whose process is alive.
A socket left by a process that is gone is ignored.
.PP
No answer comes back from the instance.
.B keyfilterctl
tells that the command was handed over, not that it was used.
A command is checked before it is sent, a mistake in it is a usage error and nothing is sent.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pid " \fIPID\fR"
The instance to talk to.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the usage.
.PP
The options come before the command.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B ls
List the instances, one per line: the pid, a space and the command the instance runs.
The instances are sorted by pid.
An instance without information has the command
.BR (unknown) .
Nothing is printed when no instance runs.
.TP
.BI send " TEXT"
Send text to the command of the instance.
The text is written like the replacement of a map, see
.BR keyfilter (1):
.BR \en ,
.BR \et ,
.BR \ee ,
.BI \ex HH
and keys between
.B <<
and
.BR >> .
Several arguments are joined with a space.
Spaces at the start are not kept, write
.BR \ex20 .
A
.B #
starts a comment, write
.BR \e# .
The text cannot contain a line end, write
.BR \en .
.TP
.BI toggle " PROFILE"
Turn a profile on if it is off and off if it is on.
It is a name or a path, without a space or a
.BR # .
The names
.B keyfilter_runtime
and
.B keyfilter_command_line
are refused.
A profile that the instance does not have is loaded and put after the others.
A profile that comes back is where it was.
If it cannot be loaded the instance goes on with the profiles it had.
.TP
.BI block " KEYS"
.TQ
.BI unblock " KEYS"
.TQ
.BI map " KEY=REPLACEMENT"
Add a rule to the runtime profile of the instance.
It applies to the keys typed after it, and comes after the rules of the profiles and of the command line.
The arguments are joined with a space and used as the argument of the command of the same name of a profile.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command was done, or handed over.
.TP
.B 1
The command line is wrong, or the text of the command is not valid.
.TP
.B 2
No instance can be reached: none runs, several run and no pid was given, the pid is not an instance, or the connection failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
keyfilterctl ls
keyfilterctl send \(aqabc<<Enter>>\(aq
keyfilterctl toggle gaming
keyfilterctl block C\-z
keyfilterctl map F5=\(aqgit status\en\(aq
keyfilterctl \-\-pid 4242 unblock C\-z
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/keyfilter/sockets/PID.sock
.TQ
.I ~/.config/keyfilter/sockets/PID.info
The socket and the information of an instance.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Where the sockets are.
.SH AUTHOR
Nadim Khemir <nadim.khemir@gmail.com>
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR keyfilter (1)
